<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Württemberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1495 CE – 1803 CE · capital Stuttgart</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The south-west German duchy, raised in 1495, that combined strong estates, a model Lutheran church and a constitution dating from 1514.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-wurttemberg">County of Württemberg</a>, reunited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eberhard-i-duke-of-wurttemberg">Count Eberhard im Bart</a> in 1482, was raised to a duchy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diet-of-worms-1495">Diet of Worms</a> in 1495; Eberhard had founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-tubingen">University of Tübingen</a> in 1477. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-wurttemberg">Ulrich</a> proved a violent ruler, and the peasant revolt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poor-conrad">Poor Conrad</a> in 1514 forced him to accept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tubingen">Treaty of Tübingen</a>, guaranteeing the estates' rights.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Expelled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-league">Swabian League</a> in 1519, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-duke-of-wurttemberg">Ulrich</a> lost the duchy to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> until 1534, when he returned and introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a>. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christoph-duke-of-wurttemberg">Christoph</a> built a model Lutheran church order and school system. The Landtag, dominated by town notables because the nobility had left the duchy, controlled taxation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> was the capital, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigsburg-palace">Ludwigsburg</a> was built as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> residence from 1704.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-wurttemberg">Duke Frederick II</a> gained the rank of elector in 1803 and, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, became king in 1806, doubling his lands; the duchy became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-wurttemberg">Electorate of Württemberg</a> and then the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg</a>. Its territory is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-wurttemberg.jpg" alt="Duchy of Württemberg" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wuerttemberg_Banner.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1495 CE – 1803 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stuttgart</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Eberhard im Bart, Ulrich, Christoph</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herzogtum Württemberg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1649&amp;focus=duchy-of-wurttemberg" aria-label="Duchy of Württemberg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4892 474 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Württemberg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5212 730L5159 768M5212 730L5212 730M5212 730L5229 694M5212 730L5381 747M5212 730L5212 730M5212 730L5216 731M5212 730L5214 739M5212 730L5213 719M5212 730L5225 720M5212 730L5227 730M5212 730L5205 731M5212 730L5235 726M5212 730L5237 735M5212 730L5201 748"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5159 768h0M5212 730h0M5229 694h0M5381 747h0M5212 730h0M5216 731h0M5214 739h0M52